    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "The Grand Canyon cuts through the Colorado Plateau in northern Arizona. The Colorado River runs 446 km through it, and the canyon is up to 29 km wide. The South Rim stands near 2,100 m and the North Rim about 300 m higher.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "The walls expose close to two billion years of rock, from Vishnu Schist at the river to Kaibab limestone at the rim. Eleven tribes, including the Havasupai, Hualapai and Navajo, have ties to the canyon. Theodore Roosevelt protected it in 1908, and it became a national park in 1919.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "March to May and September to November bring cooler hiking weather. The South Rim is open all year, while North Rim services close from mid-October to mid-May. Carry more water than you think: the inner canyon passes 40°C in summer.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
